= Pear (Pyrus spp.)

_Scope: This entry treats the pear — the European pear Pyrus communis L. and the East-Asian pears (P. pyrifolia, the sand/Nashi pear; P. bretschneideri, the ya pear; P. ussuriensis), all Rosaceae — and its fruit. Pear is the sister pome of the apple (see `apple.typ`), and the two are treated in parallel; but the classical weighting is reversed. Pear is deeply classical in Chinese medicine — 梨 (lí) is one of the great Lung-moistening, phlegm-cooling, thirst-quenching fruits, the star of this entry — and classical in Greco-Roman medicine (pirum) and Tibb (كمثرى kummathrā); it is post-classical in Ayurveda (the Persian-derived nāshpātī), whose section is reasoned extension, flagged as such._

*Relation to sister fruit:* pear ← Rosaceae pome, sister to apple (`apple.typ`). The traditions grade the two closely and read pear as the *more cooling-moistening* of the pair — the Lung-and-dryness fruit — where apple is the more cardiac-and-balancing.

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Pyrus communis_ L. (European pear) and the East-Asian _P. pyrifolia_ (Burm.f.) Nakai (sand/Nashi pear), _P. bretschneideri_ Rehder (ya pear), and _P. ussuriensis_ Maxim.; family Rosaceae, tribe Maleae (the pome group, with apple and quince). Latin _pirum_ (the fruit), _pirus_ (the tree); the Greek is *ἄπιον (ápion)*. The word's origin is *uncertain* — like several Mediterranean fruit-names it is probably a *pre-Indo-European substrate borrowing*, shared between Greek and Latin rather than inherited.#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _pirum_, _pirus_); de Vaan, _Etymological Dictionary of Latin_ (s.v. _pirus_) — flags the word as probably a Mediterranean substrate borrowing, related to Greek _ápion_ but not by regular inheritance; Beekes (s.v. ἄπιον) concurs on pre-Greek origin.]

*English:* _Pear_ — from Old English *pere / peru*, an early West-Germanic borrowing from Vulgar Latin _pira_ (the plural of _pirum_, reinterpreted as a feminine singular), from Latin _pirum_. So "pear" is the same Mediterranean word, taken into Germanic with Roman fruit-growing.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "pear, n."): OE _pere_/_peru_ < West Germanic \*_pira_ < Vulgar Latin _pira_ (plural of _pirum_ taken as feminine singular). A Latin loan accompanying Roman pomiculture.]

*Chinese:* *梨 (lí)* — pear, a *deeply classical and central* fruit-drug. Named forms and cultivars: *雪梨 (xuělí, "snow pear")* and *白梨 (báilí, "white pear," _P. bretschneideri_ group)*, *鴨梨 (yālí, "duck pear")*, *沙梨 (shālí, "sand pear," _P. pyrifolia_)*, *秋子梨 (qiūzilí, _P. ussuriensis_)*, and the famed *萊陽梨 (Láiyáng lí)* and *碭山梨 (Dàngshān lí)* regional pears. The 梨 has a full _Bencao_ standing (unlike the modern apple).#footnote[梨 (_lí_) and its cultivar-names (雪梨, 白梨, 鴨梨, 沙梨, 秋子梨) with full classical standing: Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 梨 entry. Species mapping (_P. pyrifolia_ = 沙梨; _P. bretschneideri_ = 白梨/鴨梨 group; _P. ussuriensis_ = 秋子梨): standard pomology.]

*Sanskrit:* *No core-classical name.* The pear is *post-classical* in India; the modern name *नाशपाती (nāshpātī)* is a *Persian loan* (ناشپاتی). Treated as reasoned-only below.#footnote[The pear is not in the classical saṃhitā fruit-lists; _nāshpātī_ is Persian-derived. The dravyaguna reading here is reasoned extension — flagged.]

*Arabic / Persian / Urdu (Tibb):* *كمّثرى (kummathrā)* — pear, a *classical* Tibb fruit. Persian: *گلابی (golābī)*, also *امرود (amrūd)*. *Nomenclature caution:* Persian/older Urdu *امرود (amrūd)* meant *pear*, but in *modern Urdu امرود now denotes guava* — a real false-friend; the unambiguous Urdu for pear is *ناشپاتی (nāshpātī)*. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.#footnote[_Kummathrā_ (pear) as a classical Tibb fruit: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II (_kummathrā_); Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_. The Persian/Urdu _amrūd_ shift (historically "pear," now "guava" in modern Urdu) is a documented semantic change — flagged to prevent misidentification; modern Urdu pear = _nāshpātī_.]

*Greek:* *ἄπιον (ápion)* — pear; *ἀχράς (achrás)* — the wild pear.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_This is the substantive classical tradition for the pear._ 梨 _(lí) is a canonical_ Bencao _fruit-drug and the paradigmatic_ Lung-moistening, heat-clearing, fluid-generating _fruit — the fruit of autumn dryness (秋燥) and of dry cough. Fully classical, not reasoned._

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Cool (涼); the *cooked* pear (stewed with rock sugar) is gentler and more neutral-moistening.

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘), slightly sour (微酸).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Lung (肺), Stomach (胃); some sources add Heart (心).#footnote[Property (cool), flavor (sweet, slightly sour), and Lung/Stomach channels of 梨: Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_, 梨 entry; systematized in modern Chinese materia medica (cf. Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed., dietetic sections).]

*Actions & indications:*
- *清熱潤肺 (qīng rè rùn fèi)* — *clears heat and moistens the Lung* — *the signature action*; for dry cough, Lung-heat, and *秋燥 (qiūzào,* autumn dryness) with dry throat, hoarseness, and dry nose.
- *生津止渴 (shēng jīn zhǐ kě)* — generates fluids and quenches thirst; for heat-damage-to-fluids, febrile thirst, and _xiāokě_ (消渴, wasting-thirst / diabetes-like patterns).
- *化痰止咳 (huà tán zhǐ ké)* — transforms (hot/dry) phlegm and stops cough; the basis of the pear-based cough syrups.
- *除煩 (chú fán)* — clears heat-vexation and restlessness; *解酒毒 (jiě jiǔ dú)* — resolves alcohol toxicity (the classical hangover use); *潤燥通便 (rùn zào tōng biàn)* — moistens dryness and eases dry constitution.

*Cautions:* the cool-moist nature is *contraindicated in Spleen-Stomach cold-vacuity* (loose stools, cold abdomen, poor appetite), in *cold-pattern cough* (clear/white phlegm), in post-partum and after childbirth cold states, and in the chilled/raw form for those constitutions — for which the *cooked* pear (stewed, with warming adjuncts) is used instead. The classical saying warns the raw pear is cold and "not to be eaten much" by the cold-and-weak.#footnote[Actions (清熱潤肺, 生津止渴, 化痰止咳, 除煩, 解酒), the autumn-dryness (秋燥) indication, and the Spleen-cold/cold-cough/post-partum cautions: _Bencao Gangmu_, 梨 entry; standard Chinese dietetics. The raw-cool vs cooked-gentler distinction is classical. See the 潤 (rùn) and 清 (qīng) glossary entries.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical._ Pyrus _is post-classical in India (the Persian-derived nāshpātī) and absent from the core saṃhitā fruit-lists. The reading below applies dravyaguna principles by extension, in the register of the other reasoned-only entries and closely parallel to the apple (`apple.typ`)._

*Reasoned rasa:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent — pronounced, from the tannin and stone-cells), slight Amla.

*Reasoned guṇa:* Guru (गुरु, heavy — the pear is denser/heavier than the apple), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry, from the astringency), Sīta-sparśa (cooling).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Śīta (शीत, cooling).

*Reasoned vipāka:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).

*Prabhāva:* None classically (no classical entry). If singled out, the marked *demulcent Lung-moistening yet astringent* combination — moistening the dry airway while binding the bowel — is the candidate feature; modern interpretive, flagged (and it is exactly the Chinese 潤肺 + 澀 reading in dravyaguna dress).

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pitta-śāmaka (sweet-cooling-astringent — a strong Pitta pacifier, converging with the Chinese heat-clearing reading) and Kapha-neutral-to-reducing (the astringent-dry note); *Vāta-vardhaka when raw* (the guru-rūkṣa-śīta-kaṣāya profile) — hence, as with apple, the reasoned counsel that *raw pear aggravates Vāta and is best stewed* with warming spices.

*Reasoned karma:* _Grāhī_ (astringent, stool-binding), _pitta-śāmaka, dāha-praśamana_ (relieves burning), _tṛṣṇā-nigrahaṇa_ (thirst-quenching), _kāsahara_ (anti-cough, esp. dry cough — the 潤肺止咳 convergence), _mūtrala_ (mild diuretic). Cooked pear is _laghu-bṛṃhaṇa_, the convalescent and infant form.#footnote[Reasoned placement; not classical. The reading closely tracks the apple's, shifted toward stronger astringency (stone-cells/tannin) and a more pronounced cooling-Lung-moistening register that maps onto the Chinese 清熱潤肺; the raw-aggravates-Vāta/cooked-pacifies teaching applies by extension — flagged. _Grāhī_, _tṛṣṇā-nigrahaṇa_ per their glossary entries.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(no classical viruddha for the post-classical pear — extensions of conditional rules; tags S/N/F/M)_

- *(extended) Conditional:* Vāta excess and cold-rough digestion (raw, cold pear); Spleen-cold/loose-stool states (the 中醫 caution in dravyaguna terms); the _phala + dugdha_ fruit-with-milk folk caution for the raw fruit.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *FODMAP / sorbitol* — pears are *notably high in sorbitol and fructose*; a common cause of bloating and osmotic (sometimes explosive) diarrhea in FODMAP-sensitive people — which is precisely why pear (or pear juice) is a *traditional gentle pediatric laxative*, a genuine dose-dependent double edge.
  - *Oral allergy syndrome* — pear (Pyr c 1) cross-reacts with birch pollen, like apple (but pear is otherwise one of the *least allergenic* fruits — a classic early weaning fruit).
  - *Dental acid / pesticide residue* — mild, as for apple.#footnote[Pear's high sorbitol/fructose content (hence both the FODMAP-intolerance issue and the traditional gentle-laxative use, especially pediatric), the low overall allergenicity with birch-associated OAS exception (Pyr c 1), and residue/dental notes are modern food-science overlays — flagged (M).]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Pear (*كمّثرى kummathrā*) is *classical* in Tibb — a *cooling, astringent stomachic and cardiac*, cooler and drier than the apple. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.

*Arabic:* *كمّثرى (kummathrā)*; Persian *گلابی (golābī)* / *امرود (amrūd,* historically pear); Urdu/Hakeemi *ناشپاتی (nāshpātī)* (modern Urdu _amrūd_ = guava — see the nomenclature caution above).

*Mizāj:* *Cold and Dry* (generally 1st–2nd degree) — the astringent, cooling pome; colder and drier than the apple.#footnote[Mizāj (Cold-Dry) and actions of _kummathrā_: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II (_kummathrā_); Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_. Degree assignments vary by variety/edition — flagged for verification.]

*Afʿāl (actions):*
- *Qābiḍ* — astringent; *muqawwī al-maʿida* — stomach tonic (the astringent stomach-strengthening for bilious, lax, or nauseous stomachs).
- *Mufarriḥ / muqawwī al-qalb* (milder than apple) — cooling cardiac/gladdening.
- *Mubarrid / musakkin li-l-ʿaṭash* — refrigerant, thirst-quenching (febrile heat and thirst).
- *Ḥābis* (mild) — checking of loose bilious flux (the astringent bowel action).

*Istemālāt (indications):* bilious, hot, or lax stomach; thirst and febrile heat; hot palpitation; bilious nausea and vomiting. The *cooked/stewed* pear was used to make the cold-dry raw fruit gentler.

*Mawāneʿ / Iḥtirāzāt (contraindications):* cold constitutions and cold stomachs (the Cold-Dry mizāj); excess raw pear is held to generate colic and be slow/heavy to digest — hence the counsel to eat it ripe or cooked and to follow it with a warming aromatic.

*Muṣliḥ (correctives):* honey, and warm aromatics (cinnamon, clove, aniseed) — the spicing of stewed pear and pear preserves. *Badal (substitute):* apple (_tuffāḥ_) and quince (_safarjal_) for the astringent-cardiac-stomachic register.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* pear is a *Greco-Arabic (Avicennan) drug*, not a Qurʾānic or firmly-hadith substance — flagged.

*Classical Tibb preparations:* *Murabbā-e Nāshpātī* (pear preserve) and stewed/cooked pear; pear as a cooling adjunct in bilious-stomach and febrile regimens.#footnote[_Murabbā_ (preserve) and stewed-pear use, and the cooling-astringent-stomachic indications: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II (_kummathrā_); Unani formulary tradition (AYUSH, _National Formulary of Unani Medicine_).]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

*The richest preparation tradition of any fruit in this materia medica* — the pear-and-rock-sugar cough remedies are Chinese household and palace classics:
- *秋梨膏 (qiūlígāo, "autumn-pear syrup/paste")* — pear juice reduced with rock sugar and honey and herbs (often with 川貝, Fritillaria, and others) into a thick cough-and-throat electuary; a famous Beijing/imperial-palace remedy for dry cough, hoarseness, and 秋燥 dryness.
- *川貝燉雪梨 / 川貝蒸梨 (chuānbèi dùn/zhēng xuělí)* — a pear cored and stuffed with *川貝母 (Fritillaria cirrhosa)* and rock sugar, then steamed/stewed — *the* canonical home remedy for dry/hot-phlegm cough. A textbook _saṃskāra_: raw-cool pear + rock sugar + Fritillaria, cooked → a warm-gentle, deeply Lung-moistening antitussive.
- *冰糖雪梨 (bīngtáng xuělí)* — snow-pear stewed with rock sugar (often with goji, jujube, or aged tangerine peel); the everyday moistening dessert-remedy for dryness and thirst. See `goji-berry.typ`, `jujube-dates.typ`, `tangerine-peel.typ`.
- *梨汁 / 雪梨湯 (lí zhī / xuělí tāng)* — fresh pear juice and pear soup for febrile thirst and heat-vexation (the classical _wǔ zhī yǐn_ "five-juice drink" of the warm-disease tradition uses pear juice among its fluid-restoring ingredients).#footnote[秋梨膏, 川貝燉梨, 冰糖雪梨, and pear juice in the _wǔ zhī yǐn_ (五汁飲) fluid-restoring drink of the Qing warm-disease (溫病) tradition (Wu Jutong, _Wēnbìng Tiáobiàn_ 溫病條辨): standard Chinese materia-medica and formula literature (Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.). The Fritillaria (川貝母) + pear + rock-sugar steamed remedy is a canonical home antitussive. Cross-referenced preparations: `goji-berry.typ`, `jujube-dates.typ`, `tangerine-peel.typ`.]

=== Ayurveda

No classical formula (post-classical fruit). The living preparation is *stewed/spiced pear* — the Vāta-pacifying, Lung-moistening cooked form — and pear (or pear juice) as a *gentle pediatric laxative* (the sorbitol). Post-classical, flagged.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

- *Greco-Roman:* *Dioscorides*, *Pliny* (_Naturalis Historia_ XV, cataloguing dozens of pear varieties), and *Galen* class pears as *cooling and astringent*, stomach-toning but *heavy raw* — best *cooked*, and (a Roman commonplace) *not to be eaten on an empty stomach* / to be followed by wine. Wild pears (_achrás_) are more astringent still.
- *Medieval / early-modern:* *Warden pears* and other hard "cooking pears" were a staple of medieval European kitchens and pharmacy (stewed, baked, in _wardonys in syrippe_); *poached pears in wine* (poire pochée) is the classic digestive/convalescent dish descending from the "cook the pear, follow with wine" tradition.
- *Perry:* fermented pear cider (*perry* / _poiré_) is the great western-English and Norman pear preparation, the pear analogue of apple cider.
- *Modern:* pear is prized as a *low-allergenicity early weaning fruit* and a *gentle laxative* (pear purée/juice for infant constipation — the sorbitol), both of which are the modern survivals of the "gentle, cooling, cooked pear" register.#footnote[Greco-Roman: Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ I (Beck); Galen, _On the Properties of Foodstuffs_; Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XV (Loeb) — pears cooling/astringent, heavy raw, best cooked. Warden/cooking pears and poached-pears-in-wine: standard food history (Dalby; _Oxford Companion to Food_). Perry: cider/perry history. Pear as hypoallergenic weaning fruit and pediatric laxative: current pediatric-nutrition literature.]

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

Covered above: *kummathrā* as a cooling-astringent stomachic and cardiac; *Murabbā-e Nāshpātī* and stewed pear.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Pear, like apple, is a *temperate pome* requiring winter chill; the European pear favors warm-temperate zones (a touch warmer than the apple's range), while the Asian pears are the great fruit of *temperate-to-subtropical East Asia*. Pears fruit in *late summer and autumn* — and this timing is the heart of the constitutional logic. See `climates.typ`.

=== Temperate East Asia (the classical home) — the autumn-dryness fit

The Chinese pear ripens in *autumn*, exactly as the climate turns *dry* (秋燥, autumn dryness) and the *Lung* — the organ paired with autumn and with dryness in the 五行 / 六淫 system — comes under seasonal stress. *This is one of the cleanest climate ↔ constitution ↔ food convergences in the whole materia medica:* the moistening, fluid-generating, Lung-cooling pear is the exact seasonal corrective the framework prescribes for autumn dryness, and it arrives precisely then. *Traditional preparation and cares.* Raw pear for summer-into-autumn heat and thirst; *cooked pear* (秋梨膏, 川貝燉梨, 冰糖雪梨) for autumn/winter dry cough and for cold-weak constitutions — the raw-cool → cooked-gentle _saṃskāra_ that makes the cold-moist fruit safe in the cold season. See `climates.typ` for the 秋燥 / Lung-dryness frame.

=== Temperate Europe and adoptive global

European pears and perry belong to warm-temperate Europe; the "cook the pear" counsel (poached pears, Warden pears) is the western form of the same raw-heavy → cooked-gentle logic. Pears are now pan-temperate and globally shipped.

=== Modern transposition (_kāla-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

As with apple, the pear is eaten raw and chilled year-round, including in cold seasons and cold/Spleen-weak constitutions where the classical (Chinese, Tibb, and reasoned-Ayurvedic) counsel is the *cooked, warm-spiced* form — a _kāla-viruddha_ (wrong-season/raw-in-cold) drift from the autumn-and-cooked logic that is the pear's constitutional home. See `climates.typ`.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Pear is a *whole food*; dietary, not toxicological, figures apply.

*Composition (per 100 g, raw with skin):*
- Energy ~57 kcal; water ~84 g.
- Carbohydrate ~15 g, of which *sugars ~10 g* (fructose-dominant) and *fiber ~3.1 g* (slightly higher than apple).
- *Sorbitol* — pears are among the *highest-sorbitol common fruits*; the basis of the gentle-laxative effect and the FODMAP caution.
- *Pectin* — soluble fiber (bowel-regulating, modest cholesterol effect).
- *Stone cells (sclereids)* — the characteristic gritty lignified cell-clusters of pear flesh (insoluble fiber; a botanical signature).
- Vitamin C and K (modest), potassium, copper; *polyphenols* concentrated in the *peel* (chlorogenic acid, flavonols, and in some pears arbutin).#footnote[Composition: USDA FoodData Central (pears, raw). High sorbitol, pectin, the pear-specific stone-cells (sclereids), and peel-concentrated polyphenols are characteristic; verify current figures.]

*Pharmacology / physiology of note:*
- *Gentle laxative (sorbitol)* — pear/pear-juice is a mild osmotic laxative, a standard pediatric constipation measure; the same sorbitol drives FODMAP bloating in the susceptible (a dose-dependent double edge).
- *Demulcent / antitussive (traditional)* — the folk and classical Chinese Lung-moistening cough use; empirically long-attested, mechanism plausibly the sugars/pectin soothing the airway plus fluid restoration; clinical evidence is modest and the effect is often potentiated by the co-cooked Fritillaria/honey rather than pear alone.
- *Hydration / thirst* — high water and fluid-generating (the 生津 register).
- *Hangover (Asian pear)* — some clinical evidence that *Asian pear (P. pyrifolia)* taken *before* alcohol modestly lowers blood acetaldehyde and hangover severity (aldehyde-dehydrogenase effect) — the modern correlate of the classical 解酒 use; modest, and preventive not curative.
- *Low allergenicity* — pear is among the least allergenic fruits (hence the weaning use), the birch-pollen OAS exception aside.

*Safe daily intake / practical figure:* no toxicological ceiling.
- *Practical portion:* 1–2 pears/day is unremarkable; whole fruit preferable to juice.
- *Cautions / risk groups:* FODMAP/sorbitol sensitivity and IBS (bloating, loose stools — and conversely the intended laxative effect); the cooked/warm form for cold-weak digestion and cold cough (the classical caution); birch-pollen OAS for raw pear.
- *Reference/dietary information, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Laxative (sorbitol), demulcent/antitussive (traditional, modest, often Fritillaria/honey-potentiated), Asian-pear anti-hangover (modest, preventive; acetaldehyde/ALDH data), and low-allergenicity points: current nutrition and clinical literature — flagged as modest where preliminary. Dietary reference points, not medical advice; verify current figures.]

